            <ArticleTitle>NSD3 keeps IRF3 active.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3475-3476</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1084/jem.20171980</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this issue of JEM, Wang et al. (https://doi.org/10.1084/jem.20170856) show a novel antiviral innate mechanism by which methyltransferase NSD3 directly monomethylates a transcription factor IRF3 and maintains IRF3 phosphorylation to enhance its transcriptional activity, consequently promoting antiviral innate immune responses.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 Mino and Takeuchi.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
